## Introduction
Porous hollow structures, especially assembled with nanosheets, possess large surface area, a distinctive micro-environment between their interiors and exteriors, and specic channels for guest species shuttling.1–3 Thus, porous hollow structures have been widely used in the elds of drug delivery and gas adsorption, as well as catalysis, as supports for highly dispersive catalyst nanoparticles (NPs) that can afford superior catalytic activity for organic transformations.4–8 Template-mediated synthesis has been considered as one of the most intriguing methods for generation of well-dened porous hollow structures.9,10 As of now, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) with tunable metal ions and organic ligands have become fully edged self-sacricial templates for fabrication of metal/carbon-based materials with desirable properties.11 Nonetheless, achievement of porous hollow structures by transformation of MOFs still faces a big challenge in that the ultralow atom density of MOFs easily leads to the collapse of hollow structures in the transformation process.12–14 To overcome this hurdle, it is desirable to produce structural scaffolds to achieve the hollow architectures. Sheet-like NPs possessing large lateral surface area are generally employed as structural scaffolds, and thereby some successful examples
The change of atom density induced structural collapse in the transformation process from metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) to their inorganic counterparts is a major challenge to the achievement of porous hollow structures. Herein, we develop an amino acid-mediated strategy for transformation of $N H_{2}$ -MIL125(Ti) to successfully synthesize well-defined porous cages of titanium oxides (PCT) due to sheets serving as structural scaffolds. On this basis, PCT supported Pt-based nanoparticles are generated via a similar synthetic route, and are utilized to study the selective hydrogenation of carbonyl groups in $\alpha,\beta$ -unsaturated aldehydes, benefiting from the specific structures of PCT and tunable electronic structures of Pt mainly affected by doping with metal species such as Co. In this case, Pt–Co/PCT composites give $96\%$ selectivity for cinnamyl alcohol at $100\%$ conversion of cinnamaldehyde under $0.2\ M\mathsf{P a}\ \mathsf{H}_{2}$ and $80~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $3h$ This research would offer a promising strategy for important organic transformations in academic and industrial research to selectively synthesize high-value-added products.
have been reported on MOF derived porous hollow structures of inorganic compounds assembled with sheets.15,16 Owing to the more favourable hydrolysis and condensation of titanium(IV) ions compared to other metal ions,17 porous hollow titanium compounds are produced with difficulty by transformation of TiMOFs in solution. Actually, calcination is a conventional method to convert Ti-MOFs into solid titanium compounds.18–23 Based on the fact that layered titanates easily form sheet-like structures,24 herein, we developed a novel amino acid-assisted solvothermal strategy to accomplish transformation from polyhedral $\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -MIL125(Ti) with fcu topology to titanate $/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ (dened as titanium oxide) nanosheets; these nanosheets were assembled into porous cages (denoted as PCT) based on polyhedral templates.25 Furthermore, by introducing metal sources, PCT supported metal NPs could be prepared (Fig. 1).
Many efforts have focused on engineering metal-based catalysts to address the issue of intramolecular hydrogenation competition between $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{C}$ and $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{O}$ bonds.26–28 Pt NPs as a universal catalyst have been applied for hydrogenation of a broad range of bonds, although giving rise to little chemoselectivity.29–31 Based on component-regulated electronic structures and defects, the capability of Pt NPs for selective adsorption and activation of different groups can be adjusted by doping with Fe, Sn, Co and so on.32–36 Meanwhile, to improve stability and activity against complex catalytic systems, Pt NPs are generally immobilized on the supports.37 Combination of metal dopants and porous hollow supports for construction of anchored $\mathbf{Pt}.$ –metal (Pt–M) alloys may be an intriguing strategy to upgrade the selectivity and reactivity of Pt NPs towards hydrogenation. Some pioneer studies have been devoted to utilization of titanium compounds as supports to improve the catalytic hydrogenation performance of metal catalysts.38–44 Following these concepts, we engineer $\mathbf{\phi}_{\mathbf{P}^{\mathbf{t}-\mathbf{M}}}$ alloy NPs on PCT for highly chemoselective hydrogenation of $\alpha,\beta$ -unsaturated aldehydes to unsaturated alcohols that are high-value-added ne chemicals.45 Finally, Pt–Co NPs anchored by PCT $\scriptstyle\left(\mathbf{Pt-Co}/\right.$ PCT) exhibit exceptionally high reactivity $(100\%)$ and selectivity $(\sim96\%)$ for hydrogenation of carbonyl groups in cinnamaldehyde under mild conditions even without obvious variation aer six successive cycles. The $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Pt-Co/PCT}$ also gives high selectivity for carbonyl groups of other $^{\alpha,\beta}$ -unsaturated aldehydes at high conversion levels. The enhanced selective hydrogenation performance of the $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Pt-Co/PCT}$ mainly benets from dopant mediated microstructures of Pt and porous hollow support regulated mass transport.
## Results and discussion
Preparation and characterization of porous cages of titanium oxides (PCT) and PCT supported metal nanoparticles (NPs)
Nanosized polyhedral $\mathbf{NH}_{2}–\mathbf{MIL}–125(\mathrm{Ti}$ ) ${\sim}500\ \mathrm{nm}$ in size was prepared via a modied synthetic route,19 conrmed by transmission electron microscopy (TEM) and scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images (Fig. S1(a) and ${\bf{S1}}({\bf{b}})\dag)$ and an X-ray diffraction (XRD) pattern (Fig. S1(c)†). $\mathbf{NH}_{2}–\mathbf{MIL}–125(\mathrm{Ti})$ as the precursor template was converted into porous hollow structures via an alcoholic thermal process at $176~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under auxiliary of amino acid molecules, including $\mathbf{L}$ -alanine, glycine, L-lysine and DL-serine. As shown in Fig. 2, the morphologies of all samples were the sheet-assembled porous cage-like structures with a diameter of 500 to $800\ \mathrm{nm}$ . To understand the components and ne structures, the porous cages prepared by the L-alanine assisted route as a representative sample were further characterized. Layered titanate $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{Ti}_{\mathrm{8}}\mathbf{O}_{17}$ (JCPDS no. 36-0656) was conrmed by the XRD pattern (Fig. S2†), wherein the diffraction peaks of anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ (JCPDS no. 21-1272) also appeared, revealing the dual-component porous cages. The thickness of the sheets ranged from 10 to $30\ \mathrm{nm}$ , characterized by a TEM image of the curled edge of a sheet (Fig. $S3(\mathrm{a})\dagger)$ and an AFM image of separated sheets (Fig. S3(b)†). The transformation of $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{m})$ ions was very susceptible to temperature and water. Only anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ diffraction peaks in the XRD patterns (Fig. ${\bf\cal S4}(\mathrm{c})\dagger)$ appeared at $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ or upon introducing a small amount of water ${\bf\tau}(50~\mathrm{\uL})$ into the reaction system $(5~\mathrm{\mL})$ . Porous cage-like structures were not found but a large amount of discrete $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs (Fig. S4(a) and $(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{b}})\dagger,$ ). Thus, the layered titanate sheets play the crucial role of structural scaffolds to prevent damage to the porous cages.
To further understand the components and structures of PCT, Raman spectrum, XPS spectrum and nitrogen adsorption isotherm measurements were carried out. As shown in Fig. ${\bf55\dag}$ (black curve), the PCT gave rise to a Raman active peak at around $149.9~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , assigned to the $\mathbf{E}_{\mathrm{g}}$ active mode of anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , under irradiation with a $633\ \mathrm{nm}$ laser.46 Other Raman active phonons were not clearly detected within 100 to 800 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . The high-resolution Ti 2p XPS spectrum in Fig. ${\cal S}6(\mathrm{a})\dagger$ showed two predominant peaks at approximately 458.60 and $464.30\ \mathrm{eV}.$ . Both can be identied as the binding energy of Ti(IV) ${2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}}$ and $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2}$ from $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ or layered titanate.46 Nitrogen adsorption measurement at $77~\mathrm{~K~}$ showed the presence of a signicant hysteresis loop at $P/P_{0}$ larger than 0.4 between the adsorption and desorption curves (Fig. S7(a)†). The type IV isotherm with H2-type hysteresis reveals the mesoporosity of the cages, together with pore size distribution ranging from 3 to $7\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. S7(c)†). The high Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) specic surface area estimated to be ca. $375\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ is attributed to titanium oxide sheet assembled hollow structures.
The transformation from polyhedral $\mathbf{NH}_{2}–\mathbf{MIL}–125(\mathbf{Ti})$ to porous cages of titanium oxides possibly involves the nanoscale Kirkendall effect associated with $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{m})$ ion-related dissolution and recrystallization (Fig. S8†). $\mathrm{\^{10}T i(\mathrm{{IV})}}$ ions were rstly dissolved from $\mathbf{NH}_{2}–\mathbf{MIL}–125(\mathrm{Ti})$ nanocrystals by coordination of $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{w})$ with amino acids (L-alanine).47 The solvothermal alcoholysis of the dissolved Ti(IV) took place, leading to formation of sheet-like titanium oxide NPs on the $\mathbf{NH}_{2}–\mathbf{MIL}–125(\mathrm{Ti})$ nanocrystals.48 With the continuous transformation, successive shells of titanium oxides were generated around $\mathbf{NH}_{2}–\mathbf{MIL}–125(\mathrm{Ti})$ nanocrystals. Owing to the porous structures of shells, reaction species can go into the interiors to continue dissolving Ti(IV) ions from $\mathbf{NH}_{2}–\mathbf{MIL}–125(\mathrm{Ti})$ cores. Finally, the cores were completely consumed, and transferred into the completed porous cages. In the transformation process, amino acid molecules in ethanol can effectively control the release of Ti(IV) ions and subsequent crystallization, resulting in the formation of layered titanates. In contrast, without introducing L-alanine, large nanosheets were produced by alcoholysis of $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{m})$ ions, and $\mathbf{NH}_{2}–\mathbf{MIL}–125(\mathrm{Ti})$ nanocrystals were engulfed by these nanosheets, further suppressing the dissolution of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{ML}$ 一 125(Ti) nanocrystals (Fig. $\left.S9\right\dagger_{.}$ ). Moreover, when a small amount of water was added into the synthetic system, hydrolysis of $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{_{IV}})$ possibly was a prominent process, resulting in the formation of anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs (Fig. ${\cal S}4\dagger$ ). Thereby, the presence of sheet-like layered titanates acting as morphological scaffolds is a decisive factor to keep the polyhedral cages unchanged. Moreover, the type of Ti-MOF is another key factor in the formation of completed cages. For example, if MIL-125(Ti) was used as the precursor, completed porous cages of titanium oxides were generated with difficulty (Fig. $^{\mathrm{S10\dagger}}$ ).
By adjusting the amount of $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{PtCl}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ the assynthesized composites with different components and structures were found (Fig. S14†). From these experimental observations, $\mathbf{NH}_{2}–\mathbf{MIL}–125(\mathrm{Ti})$ is unfavourably transferred to titanium oxides with a decrease of the molar ratio of $\mathrm{{\bfH}}_{2}\mathrm{{\bfPtCl}}_{6}$ to $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ . The atomic ratio of Pt to Co in their alloys may be controlled by adjusting the amount of metal sources. For example, $\mathbf{Pt}_{3}\mathbf{CO}$ NP loaded PCT ( $\mathrm{\Pt{}_{3}C o/P C T}$ Pt: $3.62~\mathrm{wt\%}$ ) was successfully synthesized by introducing $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ $\left(3.5\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\right)$ and $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{PtCl}_{6}$ $\left(3.5\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\right)$ to the synthetic system. In addition, other alloys, such as PtFe NP loaded porous cages of titanium oxides (PtFe/PCT; Pt: $3.43~\mathrm{wt\%}$ ) were also synthesized by using $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{3}$ to replace $\mathrm{Co}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ via the same solvothermal treatment process (Fig. S15†).
As shown in Fig. ${\bf{S16,\dag}}$ without introducing other metal precursors of Fe and Co to the synthetic system, large Pt NPs $\left(\sim15\:\mathrm{nm}\right)$ on porous cages of titanium oxides (Pt/PCT_1; Pt: 3.31 $\mathrm{wt\%})$ ) were produced at $176^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ , veried by the relatively strong diffraction peaks of fcc structured Pt (JCPDS no. 04- 0802). Thus, doping with Co or Fe facilitates generation of the reduced size Pt NPs. The reason could be attributed to formation of stable nuclei of the Pt alloy with a smaller diameter than those formed from pure $\mathrm{\bf{Pt}}$ , and strain energy caused by the difference in size between metal atoms of Pt and Co (or Fe).54 The porous cages of titanium oxides assembled by sheets were not obviously changed aer loading Pt NPs. The HRTEM image (Fig. $S16(\mathrm{c})\dag)$ showed $\{111\}$ facets of cubic Pt. The inset is a fast Fourier transform (FFT) pattern of two sets of diffraction spots corresponding to $\{111\}$ and $\left\{200\right\}$ facets of Pt with an interfacial angle of ${\sim}54^{\circ}$ .
In contrast, small Pt NP $(<10\ \mathrm{nm})$ loaded porous cages of titanium oxides $\mathrm{\left(Pt/PCT_{\it-}\it2\right.}$ , Pt: $4.01~\mathrm{wt\%}$ ) could be prepared at lower reaction temperature, such as $165^{\circ}\mathbf{C}\left(\mathbf{Fig.}\mathbf{S}17\dagger\right)$ . However, at the low temperature of $165~^{\circ}\mathbf{C},$ the $\mathbf{NH}_{2}–\mathbf{MIL}–125(\mathrm{Ti})$ nanocrystals were transferred to titanium oxides with difficulty even aer prolonging the reaction time to $24\mathrm{~h~}$ in the synthetic system including $\mathbf{NH}_{2}–\mathbf{MIL}–125(\mathrm{Ti})$ , $\mathrm{Co}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ , $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{PtCl}_{6}$ , and L-alanine (Fig. $^{\mathrm{S18\dagger}}$ ). Although introducing metal species into the synthetic system affected the transformation of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}–\mathbf{MIL}–$ $125\mathrm{(Ti)}$ , the formation of porous cages of titanium oxide anchored Pt, Pt–Co, $\mathbf{Pt}_{3}\mathbf{Co}$ and PtFe NPs was mainly related to L-alanine-assisted dissolution and recrystallization of $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{m})$ ions and reduction of metal ions (Fig. S19†).
Chemoselective hydrogenation of ${\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\pmb{\alpha}}}},{\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\pmb{\beta}}}}$ -unsaturated aldehydes
The hydrogenation transformations over metal NP loaded PCT heterostructures were carried out under $0.2\ \mathrm{MPa}$ $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ at $80~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ (Fig. 4) with an equivalent amount of P $\mathrm{~t~}(0.16~\mathrm{mg})$ . The $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Pt-Co}/$ PCT catalyst towards hydrogenation of cinnamaldehyde (CAL) displayed a considerably high selectivity of around $96.0\%$ for cinnamyl alcohol (COL) at $100\%$ conversion efficiency aer $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ (Fig. 4(a)). A small amount of saturated alcohol hydrocinnamyl alcohol (HCOL) was detected, and saturated aldehyde hydrocinnamaldehyde (HCAL) was not found (Fig. S25(a)†). CAL hydrogenation did not occur when PCT itself was used as the catalyst. Hence, the supported Pt–Co NPs possess excellent hydrogenation activity for CAL with high selectivity for the carbonyl bond compared with the olenic bond. To fully understand the catalytic performance of $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Pt-Co/PCT}$ , timedependent catalytic experiments, which could reect the variation trend of the selectivity of $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Pt-Co/PCT}$ in the catalytic process, were performed (Fig. 4(b) and S26†). Upon shortening the reaction time to $0.5\mathrm{h}$ , $74.8\%$ conversion of CAL with as high as $97.2\%$ selectivity for COL was observed. With increasing reaction time, conversion of CAL was gradually increased, and selectivity for COL was slightly decreased. Upon prolonging the reaction time to $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , the selectivity for COL is partly decreased to $89.5\%$ . The dramatic variation of selectivity for COL did not occur with the change of reaction time.
Apart from reactivity and selectivity, recyclability is a crucial feature for heterogeneous catalysts. In the hydrogenation of the CAL process, $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Pt-Co/PCT}$ shows high stability. No signicant loss of the conversion efficiency of CAL or the selectivity for COL can be found aer six successive catalytic cycles (Fig. 4(c) and ${\bf S}27\dagger$ ). Structural characterization of $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Pt-Co/PCT}$ aer the sixth cycling run reveals porous cages of titanium oxides without obvious damage and Pt–Co NPs without aggregation (Fig. S20†). Moreover, the Pt content $\left(\sim3.04~\mathrm{wt\%}\right)$ and the $\mathbf{\nabla}\mathrm{Pt/Co}$ atomic ratio $\left(\sim2.78:1\right)$ did not conspicuously change aer cyclic catalytic hydrogenation. The stable $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Pt-Co/PCT}$ heterostructures can afford long-term high-efficiency catalytic performance of CAL hydrogenation.
In comparison with the supported Pt–Co NPs that showed high activity and selectivity for CAL hydrogenation, the $\mathbf{\rho}_{\mathrm{Pt/}}$ PCT_1 catalysts exhibited low activity with ${\sim}46.5\%$ conversion of CAL for $^3\mathrm{h}$ under the same catalytic condition of 0.2 MPa $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ and $80~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ (Fig. 4(a) and $S25(\mathrm{b})_{[\AA]}^{+}$ . The product selectivity for COL, HCAL and HCOL was about 42.9, 40.3 and $13.8\%$ , respectively. The experimental observation is different from previous reports on high catalytic activity of Pt NPs, including hydrogenation of olenic bonds based on an energetically favorable process.55 We infer that the low catalytic activity of $\mathbf{Pt/PCT}$ is mainly due to Pt NPs with large size.56 The $\mathrm{Pt/PCT}_{-}2$ catalyst was used for hydrogenation of CAL with $51.9\%$ selectivity for COL at $97.8\%$ conversion, further suggesting the size effect of Pt NPs in the catalytic hydrogenation process. Based on these experimental results, we propose that Co doping is supposed to greatly improve the selective hydrogenation performance of Pt NPs towards carbonyl groups.
To further understand the effect of dopants on catalytic performance of Pt NPs, PtFe/PCT and $\mathrm{Pt_{3}C o/P C T}$ were used to study CAL hydrogenation. In contrast to the Pt–Co/PCT catalyst, PtFe/PCT heterostructures exhibited ultra-low conversion of CAL but comparable selectivity for COL under the same conditions (Fig. 4(a) and ${\bf S}25(\mathrm{c})\dag)$ ; $\mathbf{Pt}_{3}\mathbf{Co-PCT}$ hybrids gave similar catalytic activity $\left(98.9\%\right)$ towards CAL hydrogenation but a low selectivity of $56.3\%$ for COL (Fig. 4(a) and $S25(\mathrm{d})\dagger)$ . Thus, the amount and type of metal dopant played a signicant role in determining the hydrogenation reactivity of olenic and carbonyl bonds over Pt–metal alloys.
The fundamental purpose of engineering porous hollow supports is to solve the issue of mass transport for the enhancement of catalytic activity. As shown in Fig. S21,† commercial P25 and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ were used as supports to prepare heterostructures of P25 or $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ anchored Pt–Co NPs (dened as $\mathbf{Pt-Co/P25}$ and $\mathbf{Pt}{\mathrm{-Co/TiO}}_{2}$ , respectively). Note that $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ originated from ${\bf N H}_{2}–\mathbf{MIL}–125\mathrm{(Ti)}$ by calcination in an air atmosphere. The content of Pt in the two heterostructures, slightly higher than that in $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Pt-Co/PCT}$ , was around 3.92 and $4.27~\mathrm{wt\%}$ , respectively. The heterostructures gave low catalytic activity of
CAL and acceptable selectivity for COL (Fig. 4(a), and S25(e) and S25(f)†). Therefore, by comparison with hydrogenation performance of $\bf{P t-C o}$ alloys loaded on PCT, P25 and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ supports, PCT shows an obvious advantage in the improvement of catalytic activity of Pt–Co alloys. The enhanced hydrogenation activity of Pt–Co/PCT is attributed to the support with porous hollow structures and favourable mass transport of substrates and products. In addition, we expected that the use of other non-Ti-based materials, such as porous $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ , SBA-15, and cobalt layered double hydroxides, as supports would further increase the effect of supports, but $\mathbf{\mathop{Pt-Co}}$ alloys cannot be achieved via the amino acid-assisted route (Fig. $^{\mathrm{{S22}\dagger}}$ ). From this perspective, introducing Ti-based supports is possibly benecial for the production of Pt–Co alloys in L-alanine-related synthetic media.
Therefore, apart from improvement of mass transport by porous hollow structures, the excellent catalytic hydrogenation performance of Pt–Co/PCT hybrids was mainly attributed to the change of electronic structures of Pt, as well as introduction of defects to Pt. The defects were characterized using HRTEM images (Fig. S13†). To reveal the electronic structures of Pt, high-resolution XPS measurements were performed. As shown in Fig. 5(a) and (b), the Pt 4f region of core level binding energies was deconvoluted into two sets of spin–orbit doublet peaks in both Pt/PCT and $\mathbf{Pt}{\mathrm{-Co/PCT}}_{\mathrm{-}}1$ . They can be assigned to multiple oxidation states of $\mathrm{{\bfPt}}^{0}$ and $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Pt}}^{2+}$ . $\mathbf{Pt}^{0}4\mathbf{f}_{7/2}$ and $4\mathbf{f}_{5/2}$ peaks at 70.25 and $73.45\mathrm{eV}$ , and $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Pt}}^{2+}4\ensuremath{\mathrm{f}_{7/2}}$ and $4\mathrm{f}_{5/2}$ peaks at 72.70 and $75.90\ \mathrm{eV}$ were observed for $\mathbf{Pt}/\mathbf{PCT}_{-}1$ . In contrast, $\mathbf{Pt}^{0}4\mathbf{f}_{7/2}$ and $4\mathrm{f}_{5/2}$ were positively shied to 70.60 and $73.80\mathrm{eV}$ , revealing the reduced electron density of Pt in the Pt–Co/PCT hybrids; $\ensuremath{\mathbf{P}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{t}}^{2+}$ $4\mathrm{f}_{7/2}$ and $4\mathrm{f}_{5/2}$ were similar to those in Pt/PCT_1. The difference of $\mathbf{{Pt}^{0}}$ 4f binding energy between $\mathbf{Pt/PCT_{-}1}$ and $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Pt-Co/PCT}$ mainly originates from Co doping.52,57 Nonetheless, the binding energy of Pt 4f in $\mathbf{Pt}{\mathrm{-Co/TiO}}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{Pt-Co/P25}$ is still slightly different from that in $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Pt-Co/PCT}$ (Fig. 5(c) and (d)). Thus, the electronic structures of $\mathrm{\bf{Pt}}$ are affected by both dopants and supports.
The electron density of Pt can affect their hydrogenation activity and regulate selectivity for carbonyl groups as well.26,57 The low electron density induced by the Co dopant and the titanium oxide support leads to a smaller contribution of backdonation of Pt 5d electrons to adsorbed molecules, and thus electron-rich O atoms of carbonyl groups are favourably adsorbed on the surface of Pt. Meanwhile, electron donation from carbonyl groups to Pt is increased with the increased d-vacancy in $\mathbf{\mathop{Pt-Co}}$ alloys, resulting in activation of carbonyl groups. FTIR spectra (Fig. S23†) showed that the stretching vibration of the carbonyl group in CAL at $1676~\mathrm{{cm}}^{-1}$ was shied to $1709~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ aer mixing with the $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Pt-Co/PCT}$ but no obvious change of the olenic bond stretching vibration at $1630~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ occurred. The result reveals that the carbonyl group possibly prefers to adsorb on Pt–Co NPs. Thereby, the signicant enhancement of selectivity for COL is mainly due to the low electronic density of $\mathbf{{Pt}^{0}}$ in Pt–Co/PCT, accounting for its preferential interaction with carbonyl bonds and subsequent hydrogenation (Fig. ${\bf S}24\dagger.$ .
To further demonstrate the universal selective hydrogenation capabilities of Pt–Co/PCT, we expanded the substrate scope of $^{\alpha,\beta}$ -unsaturated aldehydes to furfural, 3-methyl-2-butenal, and 4-methoxycinnamaldehyde under 0.2 MPa $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ and at $80~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ for $^3\mathrm{~h~}$ (Fig. 4(d) and $S28\dagger$ ). As expected, preferential hydrogenation of carbonyl bonds over olenic bonds in all catalytic reactions was found when $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Pt-Co/PCT}$ was used as the catalyst. Furfural alcohol was the sole product at $97.2\%$ conversion of furfural, signifying the ultra-high chemoselectivity of $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Pt-Co}/$ PCT for the carbonyl bond of furfural. In contrast to CAL, the yield of selective hydrogenation of carbonyl groups was decreased to $86.6\%$ when 4-methoxycinnamaldehyde was completely converted. As for the $\alpha,\beta$ -unsaturated aliphatic aldehyde 3-methyl-2-butenal, $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Pt-Co/PCT}$ gave a relatively low catalytic activity (conversion: $84.4\%$ ) with $87.7\%$ selectivity for hydrogenation of carbonyl groups. Their selectivity for the products of unsaturated alcohols in the hydrogenation process of 3-methyl-2-butenal and 4-methoxycinnamaldehyde was slightly decreased with longer reaction time. These results reveal that the $\mathbf{Pt-Co/PCT}$ is one of the high-efficiency catalysts for chemoselective hydrogenation of $\alpha,\beta$ -unsaturated aldehydes based on the synergistic effect of the Co dopant and the porous cages of the titanium oxide support.
## Conclusions
In summary, we developed an amino acid-mediated one-pot strategy to successfully synthesize the supported Pt or $\mathbf{\phi}_{\mathbf{P}\mathbf{t}-\mathbf{M}}$ alloy NPs on porous cages of titanium oxide while using $\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ - MIL-125(Ti) as a self-sacricial template. The as-formed nanosheets of layered titanates are the key factor for the formation of cage-like structures. The Pt–Co/PCT composite exhibits superior reactivity for catalytic hydrogenation of $\alpha,\beta$ -unsaturated aldehydes with high selectivity for unsaturated alcohols under mild conditions. The high-efficiency catalytic performance of the supported Pt–Co NPs was mainly attributed to two factors: (1) the Co dopant/titanium oxide support induced the formation of low electron density Pt accounting for its preferential interaction with carbonyl bonds, and (2) porous hollow supports facilitated the mass transport of substrates and products across the shells assembled by sheets. Our ndings would help guide endeavours to engineer other novel composite catalysts by transformation of MOFs for achievement of many challenging organic transformations with high performance.
